<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murong Chong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 386</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xianbei">Murong</a> Chong was a prince of the deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> house who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> rising against Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fei-river">Battle of Fei River</a>. Taking over from his brother Murong Hong in 384, he captured and plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 385 and proclaimed himself emperor of the state later called Western Yan. His followers, eager to return east, killed him in 386.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Murong Chong was a son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-jun">Murong Jun</a> and a younger brother of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-wei">Murong Wei</a>. His birth date is unknown: since Murong Wei was born in 350 and Chong was made Prince of Zhongshan in 359, he was born between those years. In 368, after the death of his uncle the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-ke">Murong Ke</a>, he succeeded to Ke's post as commander of the armed forces, though there is no evidence that he ever led armies.</p><p>When Former Qin destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> in 370, he and his brothers were made local officials across the Qin realm. Historical accounts say that he had a sexual relationship with the Former Qin emperor Fu Jian, and that the emperor's favour to him and to his sister Consort Murong, whom Fu Jian took as a concubine, was the talk of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>By 384 he was governor of Pingyang Commandery. When his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a> and his brother Murong Hong rebelled after Fu Jian's defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fei-river">Battle of Fei River</a> in 383, he rose too, was beaten by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> general Dou Chong, and joined Hong. In summer 384 Hong's strategist Gao Gai and other officials, judging Hong's punishments too harsh, killed him and made Chong ruler. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-wei">Murong Wei</a> still a captive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, Chong took the title of crown prince and rejected a peace overture from Fu Jian.</p><p>Around the new year of 385 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-wei">Murong Wei</a> and his cousin Murong Su plotted a rising inside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>; Fu Jian discovered it, executed them and slaughtered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> in the city. Chong then declared himself emperor. He became capricious in rewards and punishments, let his soldiers pillage the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a> region, and kept up the siege until famine struck the city. In summer 385 Fu Jian broke out to look for food, leaving his crown prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-hong">Fu Hong</a> to hold Chang'an, and the city fell as soon as he had gone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> followers wanted to go home to the east, but Murong Chong chose to stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, because he liked the city and feared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a>, who had founded Later Yan. In spring 386 his general Han Yan killed him in a coup and made another general, Duan Sui, Prince of Yan. He is known formally as Emperor Wei of Western Yan; his son Murong Yao later became emperor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Murong Chong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His brother <b>Murong Chong</b> took over, captured and plundered Chang'an in 385 and proclaimed himself emperor.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-yan">Western Yan</a></li></ul></section>
